			<div class="content" id="post-content-2260117">
									

<blockquote>
<p>Elizabeth Tate</p>
</blockquote>
<p>I see what you did there.</p>
<p>Now to read the rest of the tale.</p>
<p>EDIT: I really want to like this. Seriously. The topic of dehumanization in a bureaucracy is something I'm studying in school and I find it a very interesting and very relevant topic. I like the idea of a tale (or canon, there's plenty of room) that looks at this issue as it pertains to the Foundation. But I didn't like this tale, for a few reasons:</p>
<ul>
<li>It doesn't stand alone (which is fine, since you've mentioned that this has a sequel coming up), but it doesn't have me jonesing for the next installment. There has to be more well-defined, interesting things set in motion for me to like it. The characters fall short of me really being interested in them, the 11-12 romance and 7's psychology are underdeveloped&#8230; and I don't know that I care enough to see how you tie the plot threads together in the sequel.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>The most interesting part of this tale &#8212; the matter of dehumanization &#8212; isn't treated as anything but a vehicle to tell the story of how the Foundation split apart. The tale seems preoccupied with emphasizing the Insurgency's motivation, and it shows it primarily by stating 11's thoughts on the subject repeatedly and inelegantly.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>Ironically, the anomalous girl that incites this whole mess is given absolutely no subjectivity, and exists purely as a plot object. Consciously or otherwise, the author is philosophically siding against the sympathetic protagonists in this portrayal, and it soured me on the piece.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li><span style="text-decoration: line-through;">I don't honestly buy that the Foundation would split at the highest level over an entirely ideological level. Yes, there's going to be an ideological component of any split on the organizational level, but the ideas that people say they're dividing over derive from actual differences in material interests. What exactly do the Insurgency get out of this scenario? What are the conflicting interests that set them apart from the Foundation? Why are they siding together over this issue instead of some other issue? It feels contrived at best and naive at worst.</span></li>
<li>Moose and I discussed this in chat, and basically this was a misunderstanding on my part derived from the insufficient characterization of the Foundation in this tale. Logs get at the problem in detail:</li>
</ul>
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">It&nbsp;seemed&nbsp;easier&nbsp;to&nbsp;just&nbsp;add&nbsp;logs</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>thedeadlymoose: &quot;What exactly do the Insurgency get out of this scenario? What are the conflicting interests that set them apart from the Foundation? Why are they siding together over this issue instead of some other issue?&quot;<br />
thedeadlymoose: Scantron: the tale literally goes in depth with that<br />
Scantron: thedeadlymoose: It says why they disagree with the way things are done<br />
Scantron: i'm talking about interests in the &quot;what they get out of it&quot; sense<br />
thedeadlymoose: I know.<br />
thedeadlymoose: That's what I meant.<br />
Scantron: &#8230; I must have missed that, then. What are they getting out of it?<br />
thedeadlymoose: They get a chance to run a Foundation the way they want. For Eleven, and to some extent for Twelve.<br />
thedeadlymoose: She gets to run things her way.<br />
thedeadlymoose: She's the containment expert. She's at the /core/ of this. She feels entitled.<br />
Scantron: So it's basically a power grab?<br />
thedeadlymoose: You're thinking of this as if it's not literally twelve all-powerful people.<br />
thedeadlymoose: But it is.<br />
thedeadlymoose: What Seven gets out of this is very explicit in the story.<br />
Scantron: Then why did I wade through all that half-baked philosophical shit about dehumanization?<br />
thedeadlymoose: Those were her motivations, broheim.<br />
Scantron: Can't a story about a power grab be written as a story about a power grab and not something else?<br />
thedeadlymoose: &#8230;<br />
thedeadlymoose: That's not how stories work, generally.<br />
thedeadlymoose: I mean, you can say it didn't work, and that's totally valid<br />
thedeadlymoose: Granted, my perspective is also skewed because I know what happens in part 2<br />
thedeadlymoose: Scantron: But it's made explicit that Eleven has a bunch of &#8212; at least theoretical &#8212; empathy.<br />
thedeadlymoose: Hence why it's about the specific kids for her.<br />
thedeadlymoose: She's the original containment specialist. She created most of the original Special Containment Procedures. Like many of our early authors, she's deeply uncomfortable with them applying to people. Especially children.<br />
thedeadlymoose: I'm not arguing that the execution of this has to work for you, of course.<br />
thedeadlymoose: I'm saying it literally is in there.<br />
Scantron: Maybe I'm just too cynical to get into a story that's just about conflicting beliefs, to really connect with it and feel like it's something that can happen. Or to believe that things on the level of a powerful organization like the Foundation would be fundamentally personality-driven like that.<br />
thedeadlymoose: Okay<br />
thedeadlymoose: Those two separate things<br />
thedeadlymoose: You'll probably like the second entry better.<br />
thedeadlymoose: For the first thing.<br />
thedeadlymoose: For the second, &#8230;well, honestly, that's a matter of headcanon. It's always been default canon, though, that the Foundation did start like that, and to some extent still is like that, and it's been a source of instability.<br />
thedeadlymoose: Almost every single piece of fiction with the O5s has used that.<br />
thedeadlymoose: All of the classic stuff used it.<br />
thedeadlymoose: I'm not saying that's unfair critique.<br />
Scantron: It's an aspect of the default canon that I find less than fully believable, truthfully. Not painfully so, in most cases, but I don't think it was handled elegantly here.<br />
Scantron: That you could take organizational positions and &#8212; leaving the positions themselves unchanged &#8212; put different people in them and get radically different results, is not wholly consistent with the idea of the Foundation as a bureaucracy. Obviously no organization perfectly approximates a bureaucracy, but I find that aspect of the default canon to be more important than the part that makes&#8230;<br />
Scantron: &#8230;it about the O5's personalities.<br />
Scantron: And I'll admit that personality-driven stories writ large can be quite entertaining, but there's a suspension of disbelief involved.<br />
thedeadlymoose: Scantron: They both exist, though. I don't see why they wouldn't be consistent. This literally happens in real life. A lot.<br />
thedeadlymoose: Like, you get these apparently stable massive organizations, and then suddenly they're splitting or folding because of /interpersonal drama/ at the top.<br />
thedeadlymoose: If anything, it's the illusion of capitalism's stability that says this doesn't happen.<br />
thedeadlymoose: Since, again, it happens /all the time/.<br />
thedeadlymoose: The most successful big organizations find ways to buffer against this.<br />
thedeadlymoose: But this is early 1900s Foundation.<br />
thedeadlymoose: They're new.<br />
thedeadlymoose: They're not buffered.<br />
thedeadlymoose: Again, maybe this wasn't presented enough in the tale, I dunno. It worked for me, but maybe not for everyone. I'm only saying it is in fact realistic, and very much so.<br />
Scantron: They must have grown really fast, then, because the impression I got was of an organization that was pretty powerful with lots of resources. And I guess it's possible for that to happen in a short period of time, but&#8230; I dunno. I guess I can understand it but I'm not feeling it.<br />
thedeadlymoose: Scantron: they are pretty powerful, yes, but they're also leeching off of other organizations. I mean&#8230; maybe Troy should've gone into that more. Set up how the Foundation was /then/.<br />
thedeadlymoose: I mean, in my case, I know exactly how they function.<br />
Scantron: That would have helped, yes.<br />
thedeadlymoose: The reason Four is relevant is because Four has most of the military muscle. He's a gangster.<br />
thedeadlymoose: One of the old-style mob bosses.<br />
thedeadlymoose: I dunno how much of this comes up in the second tale, though. I just know it because Troy explained it to me months ago<br />
Scantron: Yeah, this is all stuff I knew nothing about.<br />
thedeadlymoose: Okay. Well, your critique makes more sense in that light, Scan. I'd recommend editing it in, since it's already in-depth anyway<br />
Scantron: (hardly my fault that I don't know things about the Foundation that aren't in the tale)<br />
thedeadlymoose: I found your critique frustrating only because it stated incorrect things about the story, even if you were reacting to actual issues.<br />
thedeadlymoose: I know. It just means that Troy wouldn't have thought of that, nor I, when reading it.<br />
thedeadlymoose: Hence why it's useful to tell him. :P</p>
</div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">–&nbsp;hide&nbsp;block</a></div>
</div>
</div>
<ul>
<li>This isn't nearly cool or detailed enough for me to accept it as the origins of the Chaos Insurgency. There was a spat over the controversial treatment of human subjects, so some of the O5s did some damage to get their way? I think the Chaos Insurgency deserves more, or at least a cooler story coming out of the same basic split.</li>
</ul>
<ul>
<li>The title spoiled the entire piece. Maybe I could have gotten more engaged with the piece if I didn't know where it was headed right from the get-go, you know? It wouldn't fix the other problems, but it's an issue that can be fixed pretty simply.</li>
</ul>
<p>What we have overall is a piece that is philosophically disappointing and narratively underwhelming.</p>
<p>EDIT: I should say that my distaste for this piece doesn't taint my #HYPE for the rest of Resurrection, as I can accept the literal timeline of events enough to work with it, or at least around it, when reading/writing for Resurrection.</p>

							</div>

